|> Exercise
    work=ing
    handle=_cosine_rule_

    What is the dotted line distance as a function
    of $a$, $b$, and $\theta$?

    |> Image
        src=tmp-images/e5_cosine_rule_statement.svg

    |> Solution

        Recalling the “polar distance” formula

        $$
        \sqrt{r_1^2 + r_2^2 - 2r_1r_2\cos(\theta_1 - \theta_2)}
        $$

        of Exercise >>_polar_distance_,
        !! for expressing distance in polar coordinates, 
        the answer is

        $$
        \sqrt{a^2 + b^2 - 2ab\cos\theta}
        $$
        
        !! by applying the formula with $r_1 = a$, $r_2 = b$ and $\theta_1 - \theta_2 = \theta$.
        by putting $r_1 = a$, $r_2 = b$ and $\theta_1 - \theta_2 = \theta$
        in that formula.
        
        |> SolutionNote

            This result is known as the _cosine rule_.
            Like the “polar distance” formula from Exercise >>_polar_distance_,
            to which it is equivalent, it is a generalization
            of the Pythagorean theorem. This becomes extra clear if we rewrite it
            as....

            |> Boxed
                $$
                c^2 = a^2 + b^2 - 2ab\cdot\cos\theta
                $$

            ....with ‘$c$’ the length of the dotted segment!